WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.402
For a friend's wedding in another city, you book a hotel room for two nights.

2
00:00:07.402 --> 00:00:09.600
Nobody builds a house for a weekend.

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.043
Renting computers that way is the cloud.

4
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:18.154
Today we rent a whole private floor of the hotel, and decide which rooms the street can reach.

5
00:00:18.154 --> 00:00:21.035
And here's the question people are shy to ask.

6
00:00:21.035 --> 00:00:23.603
If I call a network public, is it public?

7
00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:24.731
Hold that thought.

8
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:32.518
PayFlow, a made-up payments company, wants its checkout to run in the cloud.

9
00:00:32.518 --> 00:00:34.081
First, the floor.

10
00:00:34.081 --> 00:00:39.044
A VPC is your own private network inside the provider.

11
00:00:39.044 --> 00:00:45.293
You give it a private block, 10.0.0.0/16, which is 65,536 addresses.

12
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.056
The floor spans two availability zones.

13
00:00:48.056 --> 00:00:53.364
In hotel terms, two separate buildings across town, each on its own power supply.

14
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:58.646
Then you split the block into subnets, smaller blocks of rooms.

15
00:00:58.646 --> 00:01:04.371
In AWS, each subnet lives in exactly one zone, so we need some in both.

16
00:01:04.371 --> 00:01:11.869
Four /24s, each with 251 usable addresses, because AWS keeps five of every subnet for itself.

17
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.384
Now our question.

18
00:01:13.384 --> 00:01:15.196
The one called public-a.

19
00:01:15.196 --> 00:01:16.177
Is it public?

20
00:01:16.177 --> 00:01:18.141
The obvious answer is yes.

21
00:01:18.141 --> 00:01:19.877
It says so on the door.

22
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.643
Let's see if that's really true.

23
00:01:22.643 --> 00:01:27.412
What decides where traffic goes is the subnet's route table.

24
00:01:27.412 --> 00:01:30.512
Say public-a's table has just one line.

25
00:01:30.512 --> 00:01:32.022
10.0.0.0/16, local.

26
00:01:32.022 --> 00:01:36.155
Every subnet on the floor can reach every other one.

27
00:01:36.367 --> 00:01:37.865
And that's all it has.

28
00:01:37.865 --> 00:01:40.725
No way in from the street, and no way out.

29
00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:41.814
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:41.814 --> 00:01:44.265
With this table, is public-a public?

31
00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:46.627
No.

32
00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:50.443
It's a room with a sign on the door that says, street view.

33
00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.512
The sign doesn't build a window.

34
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:54.272
Now add one line.

35
00:01:54.272 --> 00:02:03.776
0.0.0.0/0, which means every address, goes to the internet gateway, the floor's front door to the street.

36
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.955
That line is what makes public-a public.

37
00:02:06.955 --> 00:02:07.915
Not its name.

38
00:02:07.915 --> 00:02:09.097
Its route table.

39
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.079
Same question, one more time.

40
00:02:12.079 --> 00:02:16.105
A packet for 10.0.11.5 matches both lines.

41
00:02:16.105 --> 00:02:17.543
Which one wins?

42
00:02:17.543 --> 00:02:19.076
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:26.853
The more specific one, 10.0.0.0/16, wins over every address.

44
00:02:26.853 --> 00:02:31.136
So traffic between subnets stays on the floor.

45
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:36.450
The private subnets get a different second line.

46
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:43.229
0.0.0.0/0 goes to a NAT gateway instead, which sits in a public subnet.

47
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:50.075
The usual layout puts the load balancer in a public subnet, and the app and the database in private ones.

48
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.034
The NAT gateway is the hotel's mail desk.

49
00:02:53.034 --> 00:02:58.569
The app can send letters out, say to download a package, and the replies come back.

50
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.728
But no visitor goes upstairs.

51
00:03:00.728 --> 00:03:03.985
Nobody outside can start a connection to the app.

52
00:03:03.985 --> 00:03:06.777
It has no public address, and no route in.

53
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.553
So even a wrong firewall rule can't expose it directly.

54
00:03:10.553 --> 00:03:13.072
That's a nice property to get for free.

55
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.241
Routes decide where traffic may go.

56
00:03:16.241 --> 00:03:17.984
Locks decide who gets in.

57
00:03:17.984 --> 00:03:23.911
In AWS, each machine has a security group, a lock on its own door, and we chain them.

58
00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:28.257
The load balancer accepts port 443 from anywhere.

59
00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:32.996
The app accepts port 8000 only from the load balancer's group.

60
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.435
And the database accepts 5432 only from the app's group.

61
00:03:37.435 --> 00:03:43.560
Rules name groups, not addresses, so they keep working when machines come and go.

62
00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:45.882
And replies need no rules at all.

63
00:03:45.882 --> 00:03:52.292
A security group remembers each connection, so the answer to an allowed request gets out on its own.

64
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.832
Zone b gets the same set.

65
00:03:54.832 --> 00:03:58.496
Its own app and database, and its own NAT gateway.

66
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.178
And here's a small choice that matters.

67
00:04:01.178 --> 00:04:05.625
Each private subnet's table points to the NAT gateway in its own zone.

68
00:04:05.833 --> 00:04:06.974
Pause and guess.

69
00:04:06.974 --> 00:04:08.328
Zone a loses power.

70
00:04:08.328 --> 00:04:11.037
Can the app in zone b still reach out?

71
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:14.460
Zone a goes dark.

72
00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:21.460
But zone b's route goes to its own NAT gateway, so zone b can still reach out, and keeps working.

73
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:27.490
One shared NAT gateway would be cheaper, and many teams accept that for test environments.

74
00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.725
They just know that zone b then depends on zone a.

75
00:04:31.633 --> 00:04:33.655
Now remember the hotel bill.

76
00:04:33.655 --> 00:04:39.792
Some of these pieces bill every hour just for existing, even when no request arrives.

77
00:04:39.792 --> 00:04:44.773
The load balancer, the two NAT gateways, and any public IPv4 address.

78
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.352
Those are the rooms nobody sleeps in.

79
00:04:47.352 --> 00:04:51.485
A budget alert only sends you an email, it doesn't stop anything.

80
00:04:51.485 --> 00:04:54.791
So after a test, that list is what you delete first.

81
00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.491
So, the moral of the story.

82
00:04:57.491 --> 00:05:01.006
The route table, not the name, makes a subnet public.

83
00:05:01.006 --> 00:05:04.388
Every address to the internet gateway means public.

84
00:05:04.388 --> 00:05:08.036
Every address to a NAT gateway means private, out only.

85
00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:09.841
A quick check.

86
00:05:09.841 --> 00:05:15.824
A subnet named private-x has a route that sends every address to the internet gateway.

87
00:05:15.824 --> 00:05:17.146
Public, or private?

88
00:05:17.146 --> 00:05:18.259
Pause and guess.

89
00:05:20.433 --> 00:05:20.918
Public.

90
00:05:20.918 --> 00:05:25.076
The route table decides, whatever the name on the door says.
